#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221156 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221440 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a93711c-90bc-52d2-905f-80b2c1fc487b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Laplace Transforms Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "41b1a655-6092-57b6-8f95-396c566e7a83" :question/math-academy-id "e-21445" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{t^n\\big\\}=\\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-2)\\big(t^2+3t+2\\big) \\right\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a \\ge 0$ and $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}$ exists for $s > s_0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}\n= e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+a)\\big\\},\n\\qquad \\text{for} \\; s > s_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-2)\\big(t^2+3t+2\\big) \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2,$ and we have \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-2)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-2s}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+2)\\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=t^2+3t+2.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+2) & =(t+2)^{2}+3(t+2)+2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4t+4\\,+\\,3t+6\\,+\\,2 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+7t+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{t^n\\big\\}=\\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$ and $\\mathcal{L} \\{1\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+2)} & =L{{t}^{2}+7t+12} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+7L{t}+12L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+7\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+12\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{7}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{12}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-2)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-2s}\\,L{g(t+2)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{7}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{12}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221166 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221441 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57cca080-3b0b-57b9-a7f8-6b1038ebd686" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of Trig Functions Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "88ee94b3-1f6e-587a-a63f-736f9df7186e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21582" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-4)\\,\\cos(9\\pi t) \\right\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a \\ge 0$ and $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}$ exists for $s > s_0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\big\\}\n= e^{-as}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+a)\\big\\},\n\\qquad \\text{for} \\; s > s_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t -4)\\,\\cos(9\\pi t) \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ and we have \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\big\\} = e^{-4s}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big\\{g(t+4)\\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)= \\cos(9\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+4) & =cos\\,(9\\pi (t+4)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9\\pi t+36\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡9\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡36\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡9\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡36\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡9\\pi t\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡9\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡9\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{L} \\left\\{\\cos(\\omega t)\\right\\}=\\dfrac{s}{s^2 + \\omega^2}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+4)} & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9\\pi t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+81{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-4)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-4s}\\,L{g(t+4)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+81{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221205 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221442 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22ba3ca-cf7a-5a77-abf6-8620076552bb" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Form of the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "1b27851d-3630-506e-972c-b6cef8dbeae3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21864" :question/problem "Find $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ u(t-1)(t-1)^3 \\right\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a \\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\big\\} = e^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-1)(t-1)^3.\n$$\n\n \n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a = 1.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-1) = (t-1)^3.$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-1)$ with $t{:}$\n\n$$\nf(t) = t^3.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function using the rule $\\mathcal{L}\\{t^n\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}{:}$\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{t^3\\} = \\dfrac{3!}{s^{3+1}} = \\dfrac{6}{s^4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $\\mathcal{L} \\big\\{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)\\big\\} = e^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor $e^{-s}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-1)(t-1)^{3}} & ={e}^{-s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221440 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a93711c-90bc-52d2-905f-80b2c1fc487b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Laplace Transforms Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b182ef09-c0b2-530d-ad19-0704efeee9f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-s}(\\frac{1}{s^{3}} + \\frac{1}{s^{2}} + \\frac{1}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220171} {:answer/id #uuid "10c696cf-74b5-5afc-a550-86183ff0b95a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-s}(\\frac{1}{s^{3}} - \\frac{1}{s^{2}} - \\frac{2}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220172} {:answer/id #uuid "118120e0-0da1-5ffc-856a-4cfa8146079d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{2}{s^{2}} + \\frac{2}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220173} {:answer/id #uuid "61a28c1e-d045-517c-be91-fc1e10a75b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} + \\frac{2}{s^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220174} {:answer/id #uuid "625ab57d-5a40-582f-8f20-af9739845470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2s}(\\frac{2}{s^{3}} - \\frac{2}{s^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220175}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5d5c96bc-6dbc-5836-9d52-e4d31072c4c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220170}] :question/id #uuid "07fc3c3a-eefd-5543-8284-0927f58d76cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-326384" :question/problem "Given that $L{t^{n}} = \\frac{n!}{s^{n + 1}}$ for $s > 0$, find $L{u(t - 1)(t^{2} + 1)}$, where $u(t)$ is the unit step function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221440 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a93711c-90bc-52d2-905f-80b2c1fc487b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Laplace Transforms Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c517300-501c-5f66-8dd1-40ee6f04f3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{33}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302738} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff16169-dbd7-53d6-a0f6-015ab8384793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{33}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302757} {:answer/id #uuid "46c314d9-669a-5794-b001-364e7fb33a4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302905} {:answer/id #uuid "a50dc0f5-e95d-558b-a189-82772df5145d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303471} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a6ef5c-1c49-5828-946b-daf17f03eafc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6s}(\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{-1}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ff16169-dbd7-53d6-a0f6-015ab8384793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{33}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302757} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d5ff6dd-95cc-5416-ae98-7823d1849772" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f92e95f-43ab-59a9-949c-e31e679c7c82" :question/math-academy-id "q-331017" :question/problem "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $L{u(t-6)({t}^{2}-t+3)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-6)({t}^{2}-t+3)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=6,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-6)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-6s}\\,L{g(t+6)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}-t+3.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+6) & =(t+6)^{2}-(t+6)+3 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+12t+36\\,-\\,t-6\\,+\\,3 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+11t+33.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L{1}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+6)} & =L{{t}^{2}+11t+33} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+11L{t}+33L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+11\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+33\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{33}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-6)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-6s}\\,L{g(t+6)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-6s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{11}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{33}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221440 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a93711c-90bc-52d2-905f-80b2c1fc487b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Laplace Transforms Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b22c3c8-246d-53fa-a3d3-a39b18ba2cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{104}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302729} {:answer/id #uuid "4919f7b1-698d-527f-9dac-092a0992e68e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}-\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302915} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4599f2-8b8d-5d66-8234-d3c49a7ef211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-11s}(\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{-2}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303248} {:answer/id #uuid "977ad4c6-cdb9-5ca0-b4b7-e17e3149ff0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{104}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303395} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3cf0aa-e5c0-5aa5-93c4-602a5f1c27e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b22c3c8-246d-53fa-a3d3-a39b18ba2cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{104}{s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302729} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc980550-ee73-5cb8-8125-19a252a97e29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2f62fcb-3c2f-57e7-b9f2-f62f3e2f0d4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-331025" :question/problem "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find $L{u(t-11)({t}^{2}-2t+5)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-11)({t}^{2}-2t+5)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=11,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-11)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-11s}\\,L{g(t+11)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)={t}^{2}-2t+5.$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+11) & =(t+11)^{2}-2(t+11)+5 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+22t+121\\,-\\,2t-22\\,+\\,5 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+20t+104.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ and $L{1}=\\frac{1}{s}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+11)} & =L{{t}^{2}+20t+104} \\\\  & =L{{t}^{2}}+20L{t}+104L{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+20\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+104\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{104}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-11)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-11s}\\,L{g(t+11)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-11s}(\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{20}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{104}{s}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304879 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221441 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57cca080-3b0b-57b9-a7f8-6b1038ebd686" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of Trig Functions Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73615501-75a8-5a0d-ae42-5a5baa81c031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303153} {:answer/id #uuid "9933a85c-b338-5c59-bc0f-c6ccaa03146f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303406} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d4e5af-f5b3-5d8c-9eff-4d717e22b60d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303497} {:answer/id #uuid "cf25f9c3-58af-52c3-ac83-c9fdad7c1e7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+4{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303734} {:answer/id #uuid "e3165b4c-3ad9-5a08-9d70-2d92c07b7f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9933a85c-b338-5c59-bc0f-c6ccaa03146f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303406} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d09784f-1642-59bd-831a-f9fb74d842e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "477fdf0a-8165-5144-ad0b-72ada4a489e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-331040" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-2)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-2)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-2)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-2s}\\,L{g(t+2)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+2) & =sin\\,(\\pi (t+2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t+2\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+2)} & =L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-2)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-2s}\\,L{g(t+2)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221441 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57cca080-3b0b-57b9-a7f8-6b1038ebd686" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of Trig Functions Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "182cce37-e813-530f-98fb-0166e11c6e42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302615} {:answer/id #uuid "5bec99c0-cf71-588b-8408-c82ee7075312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303017} {:answer/id #uuid "694800b8-f95c-5340-9666-c59d00da26e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+9{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303097} {:answer/id #uuid "8b5783a5-8163-5806-a9ad-7baa90d54d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303321} {:answer/id #uuid "9010f692-aa6d-55ba-9b54-ff7a95dd1655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9010f692-aa6d-55ba-9b54-ff7a95dd1655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303354} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bd4e453-e8db-56ba-b356-f272dfe52e18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4afa90f5-03be-5d03-a214-8d5eff7bdf1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-331042" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-4)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-4)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-4)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-4s}\\,L{g(t+4)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+4) & =sin\\,(5\\pi (t+4)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t+20\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡20\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡20\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+4)} & =L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-4)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-4s}\\,L{g(t+4)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221441 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57cca080-3b0b-57b9-a7f8-6b1038ebd686" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of Trig Functions Using the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b44799b1-1927-5693-8329-a62ff21c4c67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372623} {:answer/id #uuid "3856e06f-360b-5f6d-b125-c9544e064d64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+7{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372624} {:answer/id #uuid "36c15ab5-ca38-50de-a938-ba12d2199834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372625} {:answer/id #uuid "254f6703-144f-556e-a6cb-2fbb6f34e3f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372626} {:answer/id #uuid "333371c4-3ad7-50e4-931a-58b8c46aa5a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "254f6703-144f-556e-a6cb-2fbb6f34e3f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372626} :answer-field/id #uuid "2af155b0-620b-578d-b1d6-2eb5fec5b31b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d3c52b7-9c42-5f5e-9301-6c966cfe0aa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-331044" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-4)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi t)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-4)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-4)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-4s}\\,L{g(t+4)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+4) & =cos\\,(7\\pi (t+4)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi t+28\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡28\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡28\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡7\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡7\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+4)} & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-4)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-4s}\\,L{g(t+4)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+49{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221442 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22ba3ca-cf7a-5a77-abf6-8620076552bb" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Form of the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22d89e94-064e-5669-b5bf-e3534cd8b285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{(s+6)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372813} {:answer/id #uuid "73d40c5e-bc2a-55d1-b719-c45456a9d2ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372814} {:answer/id #uuid "87ee9220-0e8c-5f9f-ae75-fa6bd2cf811c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372815} {:answer/id #uuid "45bac146-b7c0-5338-82b3-d46eb2b99ce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372816} {:answer/id #uuid "d308ca5c-57fe-5c15-87b8-9ffa535f3003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d308ca5c-57fe-5c15-87b8-9ffa535f3003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372817} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e5323ed-8b42-56d0-8b57-d91970958a6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "beac4237-0318-557d-ae12-fae80095f2b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-342384" :question/problem "Find $L{u(t-6)(t-6)^{2}},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-6)(t-6)^{2}.\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=6.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-6)=(t-6)^{2}.$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-6)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function using the rule $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}:$\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{{t}^{2}}=\\frac{2!}{{s}^{2+1}}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-6s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-6)(t-6)^{2}} & ={e}^{-6s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-6s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221442 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22ba3ca-cf7a-5a77-abf6-8620076552bb" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Form of the Second Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b441a05-7dcd-500c-b5f3-1f8f7266d9bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315636} {:answer/id #uuid "66830cd9-77af-55ac-be6b-2d513c622c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3s}\\cdot \\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315689} {:answer/id #uuid "a94dcaee-6be9-5b91-beb8-a7cf73427afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316070} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa82261-c35f-53b2-824a-017ff4374f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316291} {:answer/id #uuid "f50aa82b-f49c-5cc4-bb7a-ff4be251ca13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfa82261-c35f-53b2-824a-017ff4374f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316291} :answer-field/id #uuid "609d2ce2-6d20-5f31-aafb-6c5d8d681cf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f31ad08-3e8d-5df3-be5d-13a7193e5c16" :question/math-academy-id "q-342637" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-3)sin\\,(5(t-3))},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-3)sin\\,(5(t-3)).\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=3.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-3)=sin\\,(5(t-3)).$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-3)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)}=\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-3s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-3)sin\\,(5(t-3))} & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
